            "properties": {
                "0": {
                    "name": "block",
                    "value": "The full Fourier transform: breaks a signal into sine waves of every frequency, and gives how much of each there is (magnitude) and where it starts (phase).\n\nFor an ordinary real signal - sensor data, sound - np.fft.rfft is simpler and gives the same information in half the space. np.fft.fft is for signals that are already complex (a point moving in a plane written as x + y j, whose spectrum shows clockwise and counter-clockwise motion separately), and for when you need the whole spectrum to change and turn back with np.fft.ifft.\n\nThe spectrum has one entry per sample. The first half are the frequencies 0, 1, 2 ... cycles per window; the second half are the NEGATIVE frequencies, counting back up to -1. For a real signal the second half mirrors the first.\n\nSYNTAX:\nnp.fft.fft <sample rate: float>\nDefault: sample rate 1.\n\nINPUTS and PARAMETERS:\n\nin:\nThe signal: real or complex values. Every input is transformed and sent.\n\nsample rate (float box, also an inlet):\nSamples per second. Only the frequencies outlet uses it.\n\naxis (int box, also an inlet):\nFor a 2-D array, which way the signal runs: -1 along each row, 0 down each column.\n\nnorm:\nAn option, under the node: how the result is scaled, as for np.fft.rfft.\n\nOUTPUTS:\n\nspectrum:\nThe complex spectrum, one entry per sample.\n\nmagnitude:\nHow much of each frequency there is.\n\nphase:\nWhere each wave starts in its cycle, in radians.\n\nfrequencies:\nThe frequency of each entry: 0 and the positive ones first, then the negative ones. Sent first.\n\nREADING THE DEMO:\nThe same wave as on the np.fft.rfft page, 8 cycles in 64 samples. Its full spectrum has two spikes: at entry 8, and at entry 56 - frequency -8, the mirror image a real signal always has.\n\nRELATED:\nnp.fft.rfft does the same for real signals without the mirror half. np.fft.ifft turns a spectrum back into a signal. np.real, np.imag and np.angle take a spectrum apart.",
                    "value_type": "str"
                },
                "1": {
                    "name": "lock",
                    "value": true,
                    "value_type": "bool"
                },
                "2": {
                    "name": "width",
                    "value": 760,
                    "value_type": "int"
                },
                "3": {
                    "name": "height",
                    "value": 1250,
                    "value_type": "int"
                },
                "4": {
                    "name": "text_size",
                    "value": "30",
                    "value_type": "str"
                }
            },
            "text": "The full Fourier transform: breaks a signal into sine waves of every frequency, and gives how much of each there is (magnitude) and where it starts (phase).\n\nFor an ordinary real signal - sensor data, sound - np.fft.rfft is simpler and gives the same information in half the space. np.fft.fft is for signals that are already complex (a point moving in a plane written as x + y j, whose spectrum shows clockwise and counter-clockwise motion separately), and for when you need the whole spectrum to change and turn back with np.fft.ifft.\n\nThe spectrum has one entry per sample. The first half are the frequencies 0, 1, 2 ... cycles per window; the second half are the NEGATIVE frequencies, counting back up to -1. For a real signal the second half mirrors the first.\n\nSYNTAX:\nnp.fft.fft <sample rate: float>\nDefault: sample rate 1.\n\nINPUTS and PARAMETERS:\n\nin:\nThe signal: real or complex values. Every input is transformed and sent.\n\nsample rate (float box, also an inlet):\nSamples per second. Only the frequencies outlet uses it.\n\naxis (int box, also an inlet):\nFor a 2-D array, which way the signal runs: -1 along each row, 0 down each column.\n\nnorm:\nAn option, under the node: how the result is scaled, as for np.fft.rfft.\n\nOUTPUTS:\n\nspectrum:\nThe complex spectrum, one entry per sample.\n\nmagnitude:\nHow much of each frequency there is.\n\nphase:\nWhere each wave starts in its cycle, in radians.\n\nfrequencies:\nThe frequency of each entry: 0 and the positive ones first, then the negative ones. Sent first.\n\nREADING THE DEMO:\nThe same wave as on the np.fft.rfft page, 8 cycles in 64 samples. Its full spectrum has two spikes: at entry 8, and at entry 56 - frequency -8, the mirror image a real signal always has.\n\nRELATED:\nnp.fft.rfft does the same for real signals without the mirror half. np.fft.ifft turns a spectrum back into a signal. np.real, np.imag and np.angle take a spectrum apart."
        },
